  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to combine periodic structure with localized perturbations to balance coverage and minimality of overlap. We consider functions constructed from periodic oscillations with two frequency components, and we optimize their phase shifts and strengths to minimize the maximum overlap integral C\u2085. \n\nWe address the non-convex nature of the problem through:\n- Multi-start optimization with Bayesian prioritization\n- Local refinement using gradient-based methods\n- Spectral constraints to enforce the integral condition automatically\n- Bounded-value parameterization using exponential transforms\n\nThe approach differs substantially from existing methods by:\n- Parameterizing functions directly in the spectral domain with bounded activations\n- Using gradient-based optimization within a constrained optimization framework\n- Employing Bayesian optimization for global search\n\nImportant limitations include high-dimensional optimization and boundary issues that may require careful initialization and projection.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nA. Representation:\nFunctions are represented as sums of sinusoidal components:\nh(x) = \u2211_{k=1}^{M} a_k * sin(2\u03c0f\u2081_kx + \u03b8\u2081_k) + \u2211_{k=1}^{N} b_k * sin(2\u03c0f\u2082_kx + \u03b8\u2082_k)\nwith parameters P = {a, b, f\u2081, f\u2082, \u03b8}\n\nB. Projection and integral constraint:\n- Period 2 is divided into segments with equal amplitude\n- Integral condition is enforced via linear constraint: \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n- Activation functions: sigmoid ensures bounded outputs\n\nC. Objective function:\nC\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nD. Search space:\n- f\u2081, f\u2082: base frequencies [0.1, 10]\n- a, b: amplitude vectors, transformed via sigmoid\n- \u03b8: phase shifts [0, 2\u03c0]\n\nE. Optimization formulation:\nMinimize C\u2085 subject to:\n0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nWe use gradient projection with complementary slackness.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Parameterization:\n1. Set grid size n = parameters['n_val']\n2. Generate base frequencies: f1[i] = base_freq1 * (0.5**i) for i in range(max_depth)\n3. ReLU initialization for frequencies, with logarithmic stretching\n4. Sigmoid activation for amplitudes: a = \u03c3(W * p + b) where p are periodic parameters\n\nb. Initialization:\n1. Start with a random Fourier basis of M+N components\n2. Apply random fluctuations to enforce feasibility\n3. Project to satisfy integral constraint\n\nc. Optimization phase:\nPhase 1: Global exploration\n1. Bayesian optimization via CMA-ES on the spectral parameters\n2. Evaluate using numerical integration at grid points\n3. Apply clipping to maintain [0,1] bounds\n\nPhase 2: Local refinement\n1. Gradient-based annealing of parameters with varying \u03b7\n2. Use projected gradients to respect bounds\n3. Step sizes adaptive based on gradient dominance\n\nPhase 3: Iterative refinement\n1. Update resolution adaptively using Pythagorean means\n2. Apply frequency annealing with linear weight decay\n3. Ensure integral constraint via constraint projection\n\nPhase 4: Candidate selection\n1. Maintain population of top candidates\n2. Use tournament selection with fitness sharing\n3. Regular reparameterization to prevent degeneracy\n\nd. Projection:\ndef project_feasible(h, n):\n\"use docstring as above\"\n    target = n / 2.0\n    total = 0\n    while abs(total - target) > 1e-8:\n        diff = target - total\n        h += (diff / n) * np.ones(n)\n        h = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n        total = np.sum(h)\n        if total == 0:\n            h = np.ones(n) * target / n\n    return np.clip(h, 0, 1), compute_c5(h)\n\ne. Validation:\n- Integral: \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx \u2248 sum(h) * dx\n- Bounds: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 ensured via clipping\n- Objective: max correlation coefficient via finite differencing\n\nf. Success criteria:\n- Valid function satisfies constraints with tolerance 1e-6\n- Objective value lower than current best\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\na. Hyperparameters:\n- Primary run: \n  - Bayesian phase duration: 700s\n  - Local phase duration: 250s\n  - Population size: 20\n  - Dimensionality M+N=6\n  - Frequency base: {0.5, 1.2}\n  - Amplitude initial greedy: uniform [0.2, 0.5]\n\nb. Adaptive rules:\n- Offspring rate: based on fitness improvement\n- Mutation strength: depends on gradient norm\n- Frequency pruning: remove weak harmonics\n- Restart threshold: top 5% succeed in 3 consecutive trials\n\nc. Useful variations:\n1. Periodic initialization:\n   Starts: [1.5, 1.5*1.2, ...] with random phases\n2. Amplitude weighting:\n   Initial: weights inversely proportional to harmonic number\n3. Artificial symmetry breaking:\n   Force odd/even components to compete\n\n5. Compute budget\n- Total time estimation: ~4 min on a single core for n=600\n- Time breakdown:\n  - Initial setup: <50s\n  - Bayesian phase: 300-700s\n  - Local refinement: 200-300s\n  - Finishing touches: <100s\n\n- Monitoring:\n  - Track elapsed time via parameter update\n  - Stop iteration if no improvement within budget\n  - Heap memory: 1.5GB for n=600 arrays\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Candidate verification:\n  assert 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n  assert abs(compute_integral(h) - 1) < 1e-5\n  assert not np.isnan(compute_c5(h)[0])\n\n- Integrals computed via:\n  dx = 2.0/n\n  corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n  c_val = float(np.max(corr)) * dx\n\n- Integration test:\n  if np.sum(h) * dx \u2248 1 (within 1e-6)\n\n- Error handling:\n  - Invalid projections: retry clipping strategy\n  - NaN/Inf detected: fall back to random restart\n\nFinal output format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n\nThis plan addresses both the objective's non-convexity and the constraints through carefully constructed parameterizations and parallel optimization phases, setting it apart from the previous grid-based optimization.",
